1
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:03,800
Who is it?

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:05,600
Dad.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:10,300
Come on in, Dad.

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:12,100
I thought you might be hungry.

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:14,800
I brought you a chicken sandwich and a glass of milk.

6
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:16,200
I am hungry.

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,000
Thanks, Dad.

8
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,800
What time is it, anyway?

9
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:26,200
Ten o'clock.

10
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,300
What are you working on?

11
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:31,800
I'm writing a story for the high-school paper.

12
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,240
Can't you finish it tomorrow?

13
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,800
No, I have to turn it in in the morning.

14
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,000
What's it about?

15
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,800
I'm writing an article on the feelings about graduation.

16
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:46,800
And ... how do you feel?

17
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:49,500
Me?

18
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,500
A little scared.

19
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:54,800
And excited, too.

20
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:56,500
I felt the same way.

21
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:59,200
The scary part's leaving home and moving to college.

22
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,600
Oh, leaving home is part of growing up.

23
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,900
Well, don't work all night.

24
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:08,700
I don't mind.

25
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,700
I enjoy writing.

26
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:11,360
Well,

27
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:13,200
maybe you should think about becoming a writer.

28
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,500
Maybe I should.

29
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:18,000
You have lots of time to decide.

30
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,900
That's the worst part--making decisions.

31
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:23,700
You'll be OK.

32
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:25,200
Good night, Son.

33
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:35,700
Good night, Dad.

34
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:38,100
"Hail to the victors valiant,

35
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:40,400
Hail to the conquering heroes,

36
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,000
Hail, Hail to Michigan,

37
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,800
The champions of the West!"

38
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:48,200
Ah, good morning, Robbie.

39
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,400
Good morning, Dad.

40
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:50,900
How's my grandson?

41
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:53,100
Fine, Grandpa. Fine!

42
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:54,800
What's all the cheering about?

43
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,500
Did the University of Michigan win another football game?

44
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,220
Better than that.

45
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:00,400
Tell him, Philip.

46
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,800
I just spoke with Charley Rafer.

47
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,000
Who's Charley Rafer?

48
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,900
He's the Dean of Admissions for the University of Michigan.

49
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:10,400
And it turns out he's a classmate of Philip's.

50
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,900
We were both on the tennis team.

51
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:14,600
Great!

52
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:16,000
It is great.

53
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,500
He's going to be in New York tomorrow

54
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:20,000
to interview applicants for admission.

55
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,500
And he's agreed to fit you into his schedule.

56
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,400
But I may not want to go to Michigan.

57
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:29,200
It's one of the best schools in the country, Robbie.

58
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:30,500
I studied medicine there.

59
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,600
Your grandfather went to the Engineering School there.

60
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,300
I know that, but ...

61
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:37,900
You said you wanted to be a doctor like your father.

62
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:39,800
Not exactly.

63
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:42,900
You couldn't pick a finer medical school than Michigan.

64
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,800
Yes, I know that.

65
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,000
Let's meet with Charley at the university club.

66
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,340
Ten o'clock tomorrow morning.

67
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:53,400
It doesn't mean you're going to Michigan.

68
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,680
It doesn't mean you have to be a doctor.

69
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:59,100
But the interview will be good experience for you.

70
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:02,600
In that case, it's OK.

71
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,400
Dad, growing up means

72
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:08,700
making my own decisions, doesn't it?

73
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:10,110
You're right, Robbie.

74
00:03:10,110 --> 00:03:14,200
But, like your Grandpa suggested,

75
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:16,400
have the interview.

76
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:20,700
And then make your own decision.

77
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:22,200
That sounds fine.

78
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:23,700
I know it's sudden, Robbie,

79
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:27,000
but this is an important opportunity.

80
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,300
We'll head down there first thing tomorrow morning.

81
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:29,500
OK?

82
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:33,700
Sure, Dad.

83
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:38,000
I want you to know something, Son.

84
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,200
I'm ... very proud of you.

85
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:45,700
Thanks, Dad.

86
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:48,090
Well, I've got an appointment at the hospital.

87
00:03:48,090 --> 00:03:50,400
I'll see you all at dinnertime.

88
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:54,000
Bye, Dad.

89
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,600
Is something still wrong, Robbie?

90
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:02,200
I'll be OK.

91
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:05,200
Going away to college for the first time

92
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:10,200
always makes one a little nervous.

93
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:11,800
I guess so.

94
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:14,800
I'll be OK. I just need time to think.
